The state of its objects: which data every object carries. In C# these are fields and properties. The class only says that every pupil has a first name, not which name. The concrete values belong to the individual objects.
The behavior of its objects: what every object can do. These are the methods of the class.
What is visible from the outside (public) and what stays hidden inside (private). This hiding is called encapsulation: other code may only use the public parts, so the class can change its inner workings without breaking anyone.



2.1.1 Components of a Class
A typical class is made of these parts (you will meet each of them in the following chapters):

A unique name, such as Pupil. By convention it is a noun starting with an uppercase letter.
private fields that store the object’s data.
public properties that give controlled access to that data. In special cases, e.g. a computed value that is only used inside the class, a property can also be private.
A constructor that sets up a new object.
public methods: what other code can ask the object to do.
private helper methods that split the work of the public methods into smaller steps.



2.1.2 Defining the Pupil Class
Each class usually lives in its own file with the same name. To create Pupil.cs, use Add New Item → Class in Visual Studio (or Add → Class/Interface in Rider):
public class Pupil
{
}
The class is still empty, but it is already a new data type: you could create Pupil objects now, they just wouldn’t hold any data yet.


2.1.3 Properties in C#
The simplest way to give the class some data is an automatic property. Both IDEs have a shortcut for it: type prop and press TAB (twice in Visual Studio). The IDE inserts a template in which you only enter the data type and the name. Property names use PascalCase: every word starts with an uppercase letter, including the first one (FirstName, DateOfBirth).
public class Pupil
{
    public string FirstName { get; set; }
}
{ get; set; } means that the value can be read (get) and changed (set) from the outside. The chapter Properties in Detail explains what happens behind the scenes.


2.1.4 Class Diagram
A class with a few properties quickly becomes long in code. A class diagram shows the same structure at a glance, without any implementation details. Here is the class definition:
public class Pupil
{
    public string FirstName { get; set; }

    public string LastName { get; set; }

    public int ZipCode { get; set; }

    public string City { get; set; }

    public DateTime DateOfBirth { get; set; }
}
And here is the matching class diagram. The box has the class name at the top, followed by its data:

The exercises specify their classes with such diagrams. The chapter UML Class and Object Diagrams explains the notation, e.g. the symbols for public and private.


2.1.5 Fields
A field is a variable that belongs to an object instead of to a method. Fields store the object’s state. A pupil could store its data in these private fields:
public class Pupil
{
    private static int _nextId;
    private readonly DateOnly _dateOfBirth;
    private int? _zipCode;
}
Like a variable, each field has a data type and a name. There are a few important differences and rules, though:

Lifetime and scope: a local variable only exists while its method runs. A field lives as long as its object and can be used by all methods of the class. It cannot be used from outside the class, and each object has its own values: changing _zipCode of one pupil does not affect any other pupil.
Always private: other classes should not change an object’s data directly. They go through properties or methods, which can check the new values.
Naming: fields start with an underscore and a lowercase letter, e.g. _dateOfBirth. So you can tell at a glance whether a name refers to a field or to a local variable. The chapter Naming Conventions sums up all naming rules.
Initialization: fields should get their starting value in the constructor, so that a new object never contains random or missing data.
readonly: a readonly field can only be set in the constructor and never changes afterwards. A pupil’s date of birth is a good example.
Nullable types: int? means that the field may also hold null, i.e. “no value”. _zipCode is null as long as no ZIP code is known.
static: a field marked static does not belong to one object, but is shared by all objects of the class. The chapter Static vs. Instance explains why _nextId needs this.

3.2 Instead of new and Set… ➔ Constructor
This constructor takes the pupil’s core data and stores it:
/// &lt;summary&gt;
/// Supply the pupil's core data directly at instantiation time
/// &lt;/summary&gt;
/// &lt;param name="firstName"&gt;&lt;/param&gt;
/// &lt;param name="lastName"&gt;&lt;/param&gt;
/// &lt;param name="dateOfBirth"&gt;&lt;/param&gt;
public Pupil(string firstName, string lastName, DateOnly dateOfBirth)
{
    FirstName = firstName;
    LastName = lastName;
    _dateOfBirth = dateOfBirth;
}
Now it’s impossible to create a pupil without these three values: the compiler reports an error if an argument is missing. Note the naming: the parameters start with a lowercase letter (firstName), the properties with an uppercase letter (FirstName), and the field with an underscore (_dateOfBirth). So it’s always clear which is which.

3.3 Overloading Constructors
Sometimes more data is known when an object is created. A second constructor with a longer parameter list can accept it. Having several constructors with the same name but different parameters is called overloading, just like with methods:
/// &lt;summary&gt;
/// The "full" constructor - every pupil needs a name and a date of birth
/// &lt;/summary&gt;
public Pupil(string firstName, string lastName, DateOnly dateOfBirth)
{
    FirstName = firstName;
    LastName = lastName;
    _dateOfBirth = dateOfBirth;
}

/// &lt;summary&gt;
/// Convenience overload for when the ZIP code is already known too
/// Avoids code duplication ==&gt; calls the constructor above
/// &lt;/summary&gt;
public Pupil(string firstName, string lastName, DateOnly dateOfBirth, int zipCode) : this(firstName, lastName, dateOfBirth)
{
    ZipCode = zipCode;
}
The second constructor doesn’t repeat the three assignments. With : this(firstName, lastName, dateOfBirth) it first calls the other constructor, which sets name and date of birth. Then its own body adds the ZIP code. This way the shared setup code exists only once. (This overload isn’t part of the 01-Pupils requirements. It’s only here to show the pattern.)
The compiler picks the constructor whose parameters match the arguments. Target-typed new works here too, as long as the type is clear from the left side:
// Pupil pupil = new(); // won't compile any more, now that we defined our own constructors!
Pupil pupil = new("Max", "Muster", new(2002, 02, 02));
Pupil pupil2 = new("Jane", "Doe", new(2003, 03, 03), 4060);






TipCheck yourself



Think about these questions before you read on:

Why can’t we use the default constructor any longer?
Why don’t we have to write new Pupil(new DateOnly(...))?
What is the value of pupil.ZipCode, since that object was created without a ZIP code?
pupil and pupil2 are both instances of the same class. Are they equal (==)? Would they be equal if they had the same values?









ImportantNo more default constructor



As soon as you write any constructor yourself, C# no longer creates the parameterless default constructor. That’s intended: if a pupil needs a name, new Pupil() should not work. If you do need a parameterless constructor as well, write one yourself with an empty parameter list.


The meaning of this: inside a class, this is a reference to the current instance, i.e. the object whose method or constructor is running right now. this._zipCode means “the _zipCode of this pupil”. Because it refers to one particular object, this is the opposite of static (see Static vs. Instance). The : this(...) call in a constructor’s signature is a special use of the keyword. It will make more sense once we cover base (inheritance), so be patient for now.
Default values: every field has a value, even if the constructor doesn’t set it. C# then fills in a default value. Value types get their type’s default: int becomes 0, double becomes 0D, bool becomes false, long becomes 0L, DateTime becomes DateTime.MinValue, and so on. Reference types default to null. If the type is not declared as nullable (string instead of string?), the compiler warns you, and with TreatWarningsAsErrors that is a build error. Relying on default values is bad style: initialize every field explicitly in the constructor, so that it’s obvious which value an object starts with.
Check your understanding of this chapter with the Constructors Self-Check Quiz.






